WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:04.680
Our immune system protects us from the
outside world. But how do our bodies

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.520
know what belongs and what does not
belong? One of the tools our immune

3
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:13.410
system uses to protect us is called the
antibody. These Y-shaped proteins can

4
00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:18.300
attach to things that don't belong,
intruders like viruses or bacteria.

5
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.370
What's cool is that our bodies can make
billions of types of antibodies, which

6
00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:27.040
protect us from lots of different
things. Antibodies have two parts. The

7
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.200
stem is called a constant region, and
the two arms are called the variable

8
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.950
region. While the constant region of
the antibodies look the same, the

9
00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.150
variable region can have different
shapes.

10
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:43.120
This allows the arms to connect, or to
bind, to intruders in the body, based

11
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.610
on how the intruders are shaped. Here,
we'll introduce an important concept in

12
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:52.730
antibodies: specificity. Specificity
tells us how well the binding site

13
00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:57.870
shape of an antibody fits the intruder
it wants to catch, a.k.a., how well the

14
00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:02.680
puzzle pieces fit together. But where
are antibodies? Antibodies are always

15
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.880
moving around our body, riding along in
our blood. When they come across an

16
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.340
intruder they are specific for, they
can bind to it. Antibodies can protect

17
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.910
us in two main ways. One, multiple
antibodies can connect or bind to

18
00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:20.480
multiple molecules at once and stick
together to stop the intruder. Or two,

19
00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:24.780
once an antibody binds to an intruder,
it's constant region sticks out and is

20
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.560
available to bind to specific immune
cells. When enough antibodies connect

21
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:33.530
to the thing that doesn't belong, our
immune cells recognize the antibodies

22
00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:34.650
and eat the intruder.

23
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.910
But sometimes our immune system doesn't
always get it right. Usually, we don't

24
00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:42.880
have to worry about our antibodies
attacking us because we create

25
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.140
antibodies that are specific only to
bad things, like intruders. However,

26
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.580
some people have diseases where their
antibodies can't tell the difference

27
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.980
between what does and does not belong.
We can call these diseases autoimmune

28
00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:00.080
diseases because the body is attacking
itself. By better understanding how the

29
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.850
immune system and antibodies work, we
have been able to help people with

30
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.650
these diseases, but there is still much
to be discovered.

